 ## Overview
-Voxel Memory Bank Allocation and Miniblock Organization is a hardware-level memory layout strategy designed to maximize memory bandwidth and eliminate bank conflicts in SDRAM-based volume rendering systems [S1, unofficial]. It solves the problem of inefficient memory access patterns during ray casting by organizing voxels into fixed-size miniblocks and applying a two-level skewing scheme across memory modules and internal SDRAM banks [S1, unofficial]. Unlike general-purpose texture mapping, which relies on cache hierarchies, this method ensures that any ray parallel to the coordinate axes can access a full beam of voxels in consecutive memory cycles without pipeline stalls [S1, unofficial]. It differs from [Volume Interleaving](2174-volume-interleaving.md) by using fixed 2x2x2 blocks rather than arbitrary interleaving, and from [Memory-Insensitive Rendering](2774-memory-insensitive-rendering.md) by relying on strict geometric alignment to eliminate bank conflicts rather than algorithmic tolerance to latency [G].
+Voxel Memory Bank Allocation and Miniblock Organization is a hardware-oriented memory layout technique designed to maximize memory bandwidth and eliminate bank conflicts in SDRAM-based volume rendering systems [S1, unofficial]. It solves the problem of inefficient burst reads and simultaneous access conflicts by grouping voxels into small, linearly stored **miniblocks** and applying a two-level skewing scheme across memory modules and internal SDRAM banks [S1, unofficial]. Unlike standard interleaved layouts that may still suffer from bank collisions under arbitrary ray traversal, this method guarantees that adjacent miniblocks in any direction map to distinct memory resources, enabling conflict-free, back-to-back access [S1, unofficial]. It sets itself apart from its siblings by explicitly handling the transition from linear memory bursts to the slice-based processing requirements of rendering pipelines through de-skewing and reordering logic [S1, unofficial].
 
 ## How it works
-**Inputs:**
-- Volume data in host memory with arbitrary dimensions [S1, unofficial].
-- View direction for reordering logic [S1, unofficial].
+The method maps 3D voxel coordinates $(u, v, w)$ to physical SDRAM addresses using a hierarchical structure of miniblocks, memory modules, and internal banks.
 
-**Outputs:**
-- Voxel data arranged in Voxel Memory such that four adjacent miniblocks are in separate memory modules and separate SDRAM banks [S1, unofficial].
-- Data streamed to rendering pipelines in slice order [S1, unofficial].
